Snowflake Time Travel & Fail-Safe — Complete Guide

Summary
Data Lifecycle in Snowflake
Active Data → Time Travel → Fail-Safe → Permanently Gone
(current)     (configurable)  (7 days)
Three Stages:

Active Data — current, up-to-date micro-partition versions
Time Travel — outdated/deleted micro-partitions retained for a configurable period
Fail-Safe — last-resort recovery maintained by Snowflake for 7 days after time travel expires


Time Travel — Key Points

Protects against accidental errors, system failures, and malicious acts
Three main functions:

UNDROP — restore dropped tables, schemas, databases
Query historical data — view data as it was at a past point
Clone from the past — combine time travel + cloning to copy old versions




Retention Period Rules
EditionMin DaysMax DaysStandard01Enterprise+090Temporary/Transient (all editions)01

Default for all levels = 1 day
Setting to 0 disables time travel for that object
Can be set at account, database, schema, table level
Child object takes precedence over parent if both are set


Fail-Safe — Key Points

Non-configurable — always exactly 7 days
Only accessible by contacting Snowflake Support
Intended for catastrophic events (hardware failure, security breach)
Recovery can take hours to days
Applies only to permanent objects (not transient or temporary)
Both time travel and fail-safe data contribute to storage costs


Syntax Reference
Setting Retention Period
sql-- Account level (requires ACCOUNTADMIN role)
ALTER ACCOUNT SET DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS = 90;

-- Database level
ALTER DATABASE my_database SET DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS = 90;

-- Schema level
ALTER SCHEMA my_schema SET DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS = 30;

-- Table level
ALTER TABLE my_table SET DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS = 5;

-- Disable time travel for a specific object
ALTER TABLE my_table SET DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS = 0;

-- Reset account level back to default
ALTER ACCOUNT SET DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS = 1;

Checking Retention Period
sql-- Check databases and their retention periods
SHOW DATABASES;

-- Check tables including dropped ones still in retention
SHOW TABLE HISTORY;

-- Filter results using RESULT_SCAN
SELECT "name", "retention_time", "dropped_on"
FROM TABLE(RESULT_SCAN(LAST_QUERY_ID()));

UNDROP — Restore Dropped Objects
sql-- Undrop a table
UNDROP TABLE my_table;

-- Undrop a schema
UNDROP SCHEMA my_schema;

-- Undrop a database
UNDROP DATABASE my_database;

Note: If an object with the same name already exists, an error is returned.


AT — Query Historical Data (Inclusive)
sql-- Using OFFSET (seconds back in time)
SELECT * FROM my_table
AT (OFFSET => -60 * 2);  -- 2 minutes ago

-- Using TIMESTAMP (exact point in time)
SELECT * FROM my_table
AT (TIMESTAMP => '2024-01-15 10:00:00'::TIMESTAMP);

-- Using STATEMENT ID (inclusive of that statement's changes)
SELECT * FROM my_table
AT (STATEMENT => '01b3f2e5-0000-1234-0000-ab98cd760000');

BEFORE — Query Historical Data (Exclusive)
sql-- Using STATEMENT ID (excludes changes made by that statement)
-- BEFORE only supports STATEMENT parameter
SELECT * FROM my_table
BEFORE (STATEMENT => '01b3f2e5-0000-1234-0000-ab98cd760000');

Restore Data Using Time Travel + CTAS
sql-- Permanently restore truncated/deleted data
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE my_table AS
SELECT * FROM my_table
AT (OFFSET => -60 * 5);  -- restore from 5 minutes ago

AT vs BEFORE — Key Difference
sql-- Scenario: Statement X inserted 10 rows

-- AT → INCLUDES those 10 rows
SELECT * FROM my_table AT (STATEMENT => 'statement_x_id');

-- BEFORE → EXCLUDES those 10 rows
SELECT * FROM my_table BEFORE (STATEMENT => 'statement_x_id');

Interview Questions & Answers

Conceptual Questions
Q1. What is Time Travel in Snowflake and what problems does it solve?

Time Travel is a Snowflake feature that retains outdated or deleted micro-partitions for a configurable period, allowing users to access historical versions of data. It solves three critical problems:

Accidental data loss — e.g., wrong DELETE or TRUNCATE
Dropped objects — accidentally dropped tables, schemas, databases can be restored
Auditing — ability to analyze data as it existed at any point in the past

It works because micro-partitions are immutable — rather than deleting old partitions, Snowflake retains them in storage during the retention window.


Q2. What are the three main functions of Time Travel?


UNDROP — restore dropped tables, schemas, or databases to their most recent state
Historical queries — query data as it existed at a specific point in the past using AT or BEFORE extensions
Cloning from the past — combine time travel with Snowflake's cloning feature to create a copy of an object as it existed at a past point in time



Q3. What is the difference between Time Travel and Fail-Safe?
