Q4. What is the data lifecycle in Snowflake from creation to permanent deletion?


Active Data — current micro-partition versions serving live queries
Time Travel — when data is updated or deleted, old micro-partitions move into time travel storage for the configured retention period
Fail-Safe — after time travel expires, data enters a non-configurable 7-day fail-safe window managed exclusively by Snowflake
Permanent Deletion — after fail-safe expires, data is permanently purged



Technical Questions
Q5. What is DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS and at what levels can it be set?

It is the parameter that controls the time travel retention window — how many days Snowflake retains outdated micro-partitions. It can be set at four levels:

Account — applies to all objects unless overridden (requires ACCOUNTADMIN)
Database — overrides account setting for all child schemas and tables
Schema — overrides database setting for all child tables
Table — most granular; overrides all parent settings

The child object always takes precedence over the parent. Default for all levels is 1 day.


Q6. What is the maximum retention period by Snowflake edition?


Standard Edition — max 1 day (same as default)
Enterprise Edition and higher — max 90 days
Temporary and Transient objects (all editions) — max 1 day (can be 0 or 1)
Minimum across all editions — 0 days (disables time travel)



Q7. What is the difference between AT and BEFORE in Time Travel queries?

Both are SQL extensions to query historical data, but differ in inclusivity:

AT — inclusive of the specified statement's changes. If a statement inserted 10 rows, using that statement ID with AT will return those 10 rows.
BEFORE — exclusive of the specified statement's changes. Using the same insert statement ID with BEFORE would NOT include those 10 rows.

Additionally, AT supports three input parameters: OFFSET, TIMESTAMP, and STATEMENT. BEFORE only supports STATEMENT.


Q8. What are the three input parameters for the AT keyword?
sql-- 1. OFFSET — number of seconds to look back
SELECT * FROM orders AT (OFFSET => -3600);  -- 1 hour ago

-- 2. TIMESTAMP — exact datetime
SELECT * FROM orders AT (TIMESTAMP => '2024-06-01 12:00:00'::TIMESTAMP);

-- 3. STATEMENT — specific query ID
SELECT * FROM orders AT (STATEMENT => 'query_id_here');

BEFORE only supports STATEMENT.


Q9. What happens when you set DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS = 0?

Setting it to zero effectively disables Time Travel for that object. Deleted or updated micro-partitions skip the time travel stage entirely and pass directly into Fail-Safe. This means:

You cannot UNDROP the object
You cannot query historical data
If set at account level, all objects without an explicit retention period will have time travel disabled



Q10. What happens if you try to UNDROP a table but a table with the same name already exists?

Snowflake returns an error. You must either rename or drop the existing table first before executing UNDROP. You can use SHOW TABLE HISTORY to check which dropped objects are still within their retention period before proceeding.


Q11. How does Fail-Safe differ from Time Travel in terms of object types supported?


Time Travel — applies to permanent, transient, and temporary tables (though transient and temporary are capped at 1 day max retention)
Fail-Safe — applies only to permanent objects. Transient and temporary objects have no Fail-Safe protection, which is why they're cheaper to store but less protected.



Scenario-Based Questions
Q12. A developer accidentally truncated a production table. How would you recover the data?
sql-- Step 1: Find the truncate statement ID in query history
-- Step 2: Use BEFORE to query data before the truncation

SELECT * FROM orders
BEFORE (STATEMENT => 'truncate_statement_id');

-- Step 3: Permanently restore using CTAS
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE orders AS
SELECT * FROM orders
BEFORE (STATEMENT => 'truncate_statement_id');

Use BEFORE here, not AT, because we want to exclude the truncation statement's changes.


Q13. A table was dropped 3 days ago. The retention period is 5 days. Can you recover it?

Yes. Since 3 days is within the 5-day retention period, the table is still in Time Travel. Run:

sqlUNDROP TABLE my_table;

This restores the most recent version of the table with all its data. If a table with the same name was recreated since, you'd need to rename or drop it first.


Q14. Your company stores very large tables and wants to minimize storage costs. What Time Travel strategy would you recommend?

Consider these strategies:

Use Transient tables for staging or temporary data — they have a max retention of 1 day and no Fail-Safe, significantly reducing storage overhead
Set DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS = 0 on objects where historical recovery is not needed
Apply granular retention periods — short periods on large frequently-updated tables, longer periods on critical smaller tables
Regularly review storage with SHOW TABLE HISTORY to identify objects consuming time travel storage unnecessarily



Q15. If retention is set to 90 days at the database level and 10 days at the table level, which takes effect?

The table-level setting takes precedence — 10 days. Child objects always override parent-level settings. The hierarchy from highest to lowest precedence is:
Table > Schema > Database > Account


